"Score each option 1-5 on each criterion. There is no NA: a field that says \"none\" or \"none known\" scores 1. Give each score a reason of at most 20 words. Score from the quantities, sources and gaps the option names; ignore verdict words such as COMPUTE-BOUND or NOT COMPUTE-BOUND. Also label each option SAME, ADJACENT or ELSEWHERE against the root question, with a reason: SAME if it asks the root question or a sub-question of it, ADJACENT if it has the root's subject but asks a different question, ELSEWHERE if its subject is different. Decide the subject from the title and summary; count root_link only if the summary itself describes the root question's object of study. Do not add up the scores. Decide only from the text below; do not use tools.
Root question: Can molecular mapping of AAV capsid-receptor interactions on brain microvascular endothelial cells determine which AAV variants transcytose across the blood-brain barrier versus remain intracellular, and for which capsid-receptor binding signatures?
Criteria:
stakes: Suppose the question this option raises were answered, either way. 5 = a choice someone faces (what to buy, build, collect or scale next) turns on the answer, and either answer would move it; 3 = the answer would refine a belief but no choice turns on it, or one answer is already much more likely than the other; 1 = either answer is already expected, or nothing would change. Do not score how famous or important the field is, or how close it is to the root question.
open: Has published work already measured how the answer changes with the quantity compute buys here (samples, ensemble members, resolution, model runs, model size, search width), across the range that $1,000 of Lambda on-demand GPU time (for scale, about 250 H100-hours) can reach? 5 = names the quantity and where published measurements stop (or that none exist), and the unmeasured range starts within that reach; 3 = the unmeasured range is reachable only through a smaller stand-in system, or published work already covers part of the reachable range; 1 = names no compute-bought quantity, or published work already covers everything within that reach, so the open range lies only beyond it.
reshape: Does the option name a part of the work that one answer waits on step after step, and a reason those steps might not have to run in order? 5 = names that chain and roughly how long it is; one answer leaves most of a GPU idle (a small state, or few runs needed, so copies side by side would not fill it); names what would have to be true of the system for the steps to be computed at the same time, in a form a cheap measurement could check; and no cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system; 3 = names the chain and a way to restructure it, but one of these fails: the answer needs many independent runs, so copies side by side already fill the hardware; one run already fills a GPU; or no checkable condition is named; 1 = sequential says none, or the only parallelism is independent pieces (copies, a sweep, one job per GPU), or a cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system. A method published for a different system does not lower this score. Do not score how famous the method or field is.
Options, one per line:
{\"id\":\"structure-matrix\",\"title\":\"Capsid–receptor interface matrix from AlphaFold-Multimer and docking\",\"summary\":\"Predict structures and continuous energetic descriptors for every capsid variant × BMEC receptor pair (GPI-anchored LY6A/LY6C1/CA-IV/ALPL versus transmembrane LRP6 and AAVR PKD domains), to test whether receptor-class geometry separates transcytosis-favoring from retention-favoring binding signatures.\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the signature-scope half of the root question — which capsid–receptor binding signatures make transcytosis-versus-retention classifiable from binding data.\",\"compute_open\":\"More parallel compute buys model runs (complex predictions × seeds/recycles per pair): the largest per-pair amount any cited source reports is 5 AlphaFold-Multimer models with up to 20 recycles for one peptide–LRP6 pair (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518), and it is unknown above that whether saturating a full variant × receptor matrix with more seeds and pairs would make receptor-class geometry predict endosomal sorting fate, whose determinants are unmapped (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10036519).\",\"sequential\":\"One prediction waits on iterative MSA search and then on up to 20 network recycles, each conditioned on the previous model — roughly 20–30 sequential steps per complex — and the answer needs one run per variant × receptor × seed, each keeping a single GPU busy for minutes to hours; the recycles must run in order within a prediction, but different pairs and seeds can run at once once MSAs are cached, which pays off when pairs × seeds exceeds available GPUs, and the nearest source that tried multi-model-per-pair prediction is the LRP6 study (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518).\"}
{\"id\":\"endosome-md\",\"title\":\"pH-resolved capsid and glycan dynamics from molecular simulation\",\"summary\":\"Simulate capsid conformational change, glycan retention and membrane-facing behavior across endosomal pH (7.4 to 4.0), extending the cryo-EM pH series in which AAV9 stays galactose-bound at pH 5.5, to test whether post-binding fate is computable from physics — the one computational result that could upgrade 'not determinable from binding' to 'determinable from binding + physics'.\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the determinacy half of the root question — whether post-uptake fate (transcytosis versus endosomal retention) is derivable from anything a physics-level map of binding can compute.\",\"compute_open\":\"More parallel compute buys simulation sampling (ensemble members and aggregate trajectory time): the largest amount any cited source reports is 3 μs total (500 ns × 2 serotypes × 3 pHs, https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics18091053), and above that it is unknown whether converged sampling would expose sorting determinants that correlate with transcytosis-versus-retention labels, which exist for only a handful of capsids (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10036519).\",\"sequential\":\"Each 2 fs integration step waits on the previous coordinates, so one 500 ns run is ~250 million sequential steps and a single run keeps one GPU essentially fully busy, while the answer needs several independent replicas per pH per capsid (the published pH screen ran 2 capsids × 3 pHs, https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics18091053); steps must run in order along one trajectory, but replicas, pH conditions and capsids can run at the same time, which pays off when the relevant conformational transitions mix within each replica's length, and the nearest source that tried concurrent pH-conditioned capsid simulations is that same study (https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics18091053), with concurrent-replica GPU throughput shown at ~5 μs/day per GPU (https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/maximizing-openmm-molecular-dynamics-throughput-with-nvidia-multi-process-service/).\"}
{\"id\":\"library-ml\",\"title\":\"Variant-effect ML over capsid-library NGS with transcytosis labels\",\"summary\":\"Train sequence-to-fitness models on deep mutational scanning and library NGS counts paired with transcytosis-versus-retention labels, then prescreen candidate sequences in silico, to test whether binding-signature features suffice to predict fate at library scale.\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the signature-scope half of the root question — whether binding-signature features carry enough information to classify fate across a variant population rather than one pair at a time.\",\"compute_open\":\"settled: parallel in-silico prescreening is already known to change which variants get tested — Voyager double-screened ~25 million candidates to select a 100k library with production-fitness r = 0.94 and transduction-classification accuracy 0.94 (https://www.voyagertherapeutics.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Cox_ASGCT2025_VoyagerTherapeutics.pdf) and later screened 1.7 billion candidates to select 15,000 (https://www.voyagertherapeutics.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/POSTER_Voyager_LeveragingArtificialIntelligencetoDesignAAVMutantCapsidsOptimizedforAntibodyEvasion_May_2026_FINAL.pdf), in a capsid region where over 90% of random mutants are nonviable.\",\"sequential\":\"Training is a sequential chain of gradient steps (hundreds to thousands of epochs, each waiting on the previous weights) and one training run keeps one GPU busy for hours, while the answer needs several independent seeds and cross-validation folds plus the scoring pass over millions of sequences; gradient steps must run in order within a run, but seeds, folds and scoring batches run at the same time, which pays off when labeled variants are numerous enough that independently trained models ensemble, and the nearest source that tried parallel model ensembles is Bryant et al., where all models benefited from ensembling (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41587-020-00793-4).\"}
{\"id\":\"cryoet-refinement\",\"title\":\"Cryo-ET/cryo-EM averaging of capsid–receptor complexes\",\"summary\":\"Solve capsid–receptor complexes (engineered inserts bound to LRP6, CA-IV, ALPL, LY6A) at epitope-level resolution by sub-tomogram averaging and single-particle refinement, converting each binding event into a structural signature of epitope, stoichiometry and geometry.\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the operationalization of 'molecular mapping' in the root question — whether a structural map of capsid–receptor interactions is rich enough to define the binding signatures whose fate-predictiveness is being asked about.\",\"compute_open\":\"More parallel compute buys particles averaged and hence map resolution: the largest particle count any cited source reports is 159,660 symmetry-expanded particles from 46 tomograms (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518), and it is unknown above that whether still higher-resolution interface maps would expose epitope/stoichiometry features that separate transcytosed from retained capsids sharing a receptor (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10036519).\",\"sequential\":\"One reconstruction waits step after step on motion correction, CTF estimation, particle picking, and then iterative 3D classification and refinement rounds — roughly 5–15 sequential refinement iterations per dataset — and the answer needs one run per complex, each keeping a GPU busy for hours to days (large-complex refinement historically took weeks on CPU clusters, https://www.cell.com/biophysj/fulltext/S0006-3495(15)02062-7); the refinement rounds must run in order because each consumes the previous orientations, but independent tomograms and complexes can be processed at the same time, which pays off when particle stacks are large and homogeneous, and the nearest source that tried it is RELION-2's GPU-parallel refinement (https://elifesciences.org/articles/18722) as applied to AAV capsids at 1.56–1.86 Å (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-018-06076-6).\"}

